Result: PASS on native amd64 and arm64, using Debian 13 libraries and Clang 21
from trixie-backports. These are unsigned local development artifacts.

Source version: 148.0.7778+ds-0qore1~deb13
PDFium commit: 72ea487e4399c44c3a53a48b104f9612ca772008
Recipe base: 8c3a11b1146b98d6d428ee13616c79685139700d
Recipe correction: libicu-dev minimum lowered from 78 to 76, as tested below.
The source manifest records the exact candidate recipe file hashes.

Evidence: qore-debian13-final-modules-20261001/dependencies on both hosts;
<architecture>/pdfium/QUALIFICATION.json, inspection-*.json and
reproducibility.json; pdfium-source-reproducibility.json records source hashes.

- Independent preparation reproduces the .dsc and source archives exactly.
- Two offline, unprivileged full builds per CPU vary paths, parallelism,
  locale and timezone. All three packages reproduce byte-for-byte on each CPU.
- Source and binary Lintian checks pass without errors using the Debian profile.
- Runtime ELF hardening and matching detached DWARF/build IDs/debuglink CRCs pass.
- The installed SDK C API test passes offline as an unprivileged user on each
  CPU, exercising real rendered pixels and text extraction.
- File verification, purge/autoremove/reinstall and a complete API retest pass.

The first qualification attempt used a future changelog date and therefore
failed timestamp reproducibility. Its evidence is retained separately; the
accepted candidate has a fixed past changelog date. No binary normalization
was used to obtain the matching packages.

This covers the packaged public API test, not the complete upstream PDFium
suite. Security maintenance, final release acceptance and signed archive
publication remain separate gates. Ubuntu binaries depend on newer system
libraries; Debian requires its own builds from the shared source recipe.
